+- `list_sessions` and `session_list` on the WebSocket: a connected client sends `{type: 'list_sessions', limit?, before?}` — `limit` a non-negative integer, `before` the `{updatedAt, sessionId}` cursor of the last row it saw — and any other `list_sessions` payload is refused with `INVALID_MESSAGE` naming the field. The engine answers with `{type: 'session_list', sessions}`, one row per conversation with `createdAt` and `updatedAt` as ISO 8601 strings, and no `stream_end`: the exchange is exactly the request and the reply. The user is taken from the authenticated connection, never from the frame — a `userId` on the frame is dropped — so a client can only ever list its own conversations. A store without `listFor` is answered with an empty `sessions` array; a store whose `listFor` fails gets an `error` frame with `SERVER_ERROR` and the failure is logged under the `ws` category. The rows are metadata only: no conversation content, no credentials.
 
 ### Changed
 
+- Frames on one WebSocket connection are now answered in the order they were sent: a `pong` to a `ping` sent after a `list_sessions` no longer arrives ahead of the `session_list`, so a client can send its heartbeat behind a request and read the answer as complete when the ping is answered. Off-lock frames queue behind each other only, never behind a streaming turn.
 - `MongoSessionStore` now writes an `entryCount` field beside the entries on every save, so a conversation list can count a conversation without reading one. A document saved by an earlier version lists as a conversation of no entries until you backfill it; `docs/session-stores.md` carries the one-line command.
 - `MongoSessionStore.latestFor` now breaks a tie on `updatedAt` by `_id`, so it and `listFor` agree on which conversation is newest when two were saved in the same millisecond. Which of two tied conversations a resume rejoins can therefore change.
 - The index to create for `MongoSessionStore` is now `{userId: 1, updatedAt: -1, _id: -1}`. The two-key form that served `latestFor` cannot satisfy `listFor`'s sort, so Mongo would sort a user's whole history on every page. `docs/session-stores.md` § Latest session has the command.
