 **GDPR.** With resume on, a conversation outlives its socket, so you own the retention bound: set `maxAgeMs` on `InMemorySessionStore` (eight hours by default), use `MongoSessionStore`'s erasure methods, and see _Control what the engine keeps in server-side conversation history_ (re-cinq/HALEngine#41). An unbounded retained store is a retention breach, not a memory leak.
 
+## Conversation List
+
+A client can ask which conversations its user has. Over the same socket it sends `{type: 'list_sessions'}` and is answered with exactly one `session_list` frame — no `stream_end` follows, because nothing streams. Each row carries a `sessionId`, `createdAt` and `updatedAt` as ISO 8601 strings, and an `entryCount`, newest activity first, and the rows are metadata only: no entries, no credentials. There is no user id on the frame, and that is the point — the list is always the connecting user's own, read from what your `WsAuthenticator` returned, and a `userId` a client puts on the frame is dropped. A payload the frame cannot carry (`limit` a fraction, `before` half a cursor) is refused with `INVALID_MESSAGE` naming the field.
+
+**Paging.** `limit` asks for a page size — the store picks its own default and its maximum — and `before` takes the last row the client saw: `{updatedAt, sessionId}`, both halves, so two conversations saved in the same millisecond cannot straddle a page boundary and go unlisted. The engine answers when asked and never pushes: a `session_list` arrives only as the reply to a `list_sessions`, and a list is answered without waiting on a turn that is still streaming.
+
+**A store that cannot list.** Listing is a store capability, not a config switch: a store without `listFor` is answered with an empty `sessions` array, and one whose `listFor` fails is answered with an `error` frame carrying `SERVER_ERROR`, the failure logged under the `ws` category and no store detail on the wire. What a store implements to be listed is in [session stores](session-stores.md#listing-a-users-conversations).
+
+**Opening a row.** A conversation is rejoined, never switched in place: the client reconnects with `?sessionId=<id>` from the row, which [Session Resume](#session-resume) reads when it is enabled — the ownership check and the replay are the same code path a resume already runs.
+
+**GDPR.** Making the socket the place a user's stored conversations are reachable from makes the list one more surface on what the store retains: whatever the store keeps is what the frame can name, so the retention bound above applies unchanged.
+
